  - id: french
    spriteImg: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7e7bac5fdfbcd384eba29814a00a2ee5, type: 3}
    linkVideos:
    - titleText: Learn Basic Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Two Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Kitty Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Puppy Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    descriptionText: A strategic mode centered around a starting double that branches
      in four directions. Players build a dynamic layout while trying to empty their
      hand and avoid scoring points. Each round shifts the advantage, and only the
      most consistent player will endure to the end.
    rulesText: '- A 28-tile double-six set is used.


      - Each player draws 7
      tiles.


      - The remaining tiles are set aside to form a boneyard.


      -
      If a player draws 5 or more double tiles, the tiles must be reshuffled.


      -
      The first round is started by the player with the highest double tile, and
      they must begin the round by playing that tile.


      - From the second round
      onward, the player who won the previous round starts by playing any double
      tile in their hand. If they have no doubles, the turn passes to the next player
      clockwise.


      - If there was no winner in the previous round, the new
      round starts again with the player who holds the highest double tile.


      -
      The first domino is a spinner, and players must play a tile on all four sides
      of it before any further move can be made.


      - Each of the four arms
      can only be extended after first playing a double on that arm. Once a tile
      has been placed on all four ends of the starting tile, players may continue
      extending each arm by matching tiles. Any new number at the end of an arm can
      only be extended if its corresponding double has already been played on one
      of the four arms.


      - If a player cannot play, they must draw from the
      boneyard until they get a playable tile or until the boneyard is empty.


      -
      The first player to play all of their tiles wins the round.


      - If the
      game becomes blocked, the winner is the player with the lowest pip count in
      their hand.


      - If two players tie in a blocked game, there is no winner
      for that round. Each player receives penalty points equal to the total pips
      remaining in their hand.


      - If a player wins a round by playing a double
      as their final tile, the penalty points of all other players are doubled for
      that round.


      - The game becomes an elimination game once a player reaches
      100 points. That player is eliminated, and the game continues until only one
      player remains. If two players reach 100 points in the same turn, the winner
      is the one with the lower total score.


      - As players are eliminated,
      their tiles are added to the discard pile for the next round.'
  - id: block
    spriteImg: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2fd9ac708a520104abbfdedc4bfbd5de, type: 3}
    linkVideos:
    - titleText: Learn Basic Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Two Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Kitty Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Puppy Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    descriptionText: A classic domino mode focused on precision and control. With
      no drawing and no extra branches, players aim to outmaneuver opponents and
      run out of tiles first. Keeping a low score across rounds is key to dominating
      this tactical duel.
    rulesText: '- Uses a 28-tile double-six set.


      - Each player draws 7 Tiles.


      -
      Remaining tiles are set aside and are not used in the round.


      - The
      starting player is selected a Random.


      - The winner of each round plays
      first in the next round.


      - A round can be started with any tile, subsequent
      tiles can be played at the two ends of the train.


      - If a player can
      not play, then he/she simply passes the turn to the next player.


      -
      The round ends if a player has played all of his/her tiles. Rounds can also
      end if the game is blocked which means no tile can be played by any player.


      -
      The winner of the round is the player who played all his/her tiles, or the
      player with lowest score.


      - Player scores the total of all other players''
      tiles pip count minus pip count of his/her own tiles (if the game was blocked).


      -
      If two players get the same score then there is no winner for the round and
      no player is awarded any point.


      - The game is played for multiple rounds
      until a player manages to score: 50 points for 2 player wins and 100 points
      for 4 players wins.


      - There is no spinner in the game so a double can
      only be played at two ends.'
  - id: draw
    spriteImg: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 72640d2dcfd86824884605401c42a6ed, type: 3}
    linkVideos:
    - titleText: Learn Basic Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Two Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Kitty Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Puppy Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    descriptionText: A dynamic mode where drawing tiles becomes part of the strategy.
      Players manage their hand carefully while using the boneyard to stay in the
      game. Victory goes to the player who empties their hand or holds the lowest
      score across multiple rounds.
    rulesText: "- Uses a 28-tile double-six set.\n\n- Each player gets a certain
      number of tiles at the start of each round:\n\t\t(4 players) Each player draws
      5 Tiles.\n\t\t(2 Players) Each player draws 7 Tiles.\n\n- Remaining tiles are
      set aside forming a boneyard.\n\n- The starting player is selected a Random.\n\n-
      The winner of each round plays first in the next round.\n\n- A round can be
      started with any tile, subsequent tiles can be played at the two ends of the
      train.\n\n- If a player can not play, then he/she must draw from the boneyard
      until either he/she gets a playable tile or the boneyard has only two tiles
      left. The last two tiles from the boneyard can not be drawn.\n\n- If a player
      draws a tile which can be immediately played, then it must be played.\n\n-
      If a player can not play a tile and can not draw from the boneyard either,
      he/she simply passes the turn to the next player.\n\n- The round ends if a
      player has played all of his/her tiles. Rounds can also end if the game is
      blocked which means no tile can be played by any player.\n\n- The winner of
      the round is the player who played all his/her tiles, or the player with lowest
      score.\n\n- Player scores the total of all other players' tiles pip count minus
      pip count of his/her own tiles (if the game was blocked).\n\n- If two players
      get the same score then there is no winner for the round and no player is awarded
      any point.\n\n- The game is played for multiple rounds until a player manages
      to score: 50 points for 2 player wins and 100 points for 4 players wins.\n\n-
      There is no spinner in the game so a double can only be played at two ends."
  - id: five
    spriteImg: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ef4df6f34fce4364089fe0d3eec8108d, type: 3}
    linkVideos:
    - titleText: Learn Basic Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Two Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Kitty Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    - titleText: Learn Puppy Strategy
      subTitleText: watch video
      link: https://www.youtube.com/live/hTcBL_bg29A?si=XGWGmgFPzZX802xz
    descriptionText: A strategic scoring mode where every move can earn points by
      making the open ends total a multiple of five. The starting double opens four
      paths, encouraging creative plays. Managing the layout and maximizing scoring
      opportunities is key to claiming victory.
    rulesText: "- Uses a 28-tile double-six set.\n\n- Each player draws 7 Tiles.\n\n-
      Remaining tiles are set aside forming a boneyard.\n\n- The first turn or lead
      is decided by a draw of lot (Each player draws one tile and the highest tile
      starts the round). \n\n- In subsequent rounds, the player winning the previous
      round starts first. If there was no winner for the previous round then the
      lead is again decided by draw of lot.\n\n- Rounds can be started with any tile
      and then subsequent tiles can be played at the open ends of the layout.\n\n-
      If a player can not play, then he/she must draw from the boneyard until either
      he/she gets a playable tile or the boneyard has no tile left.\n\n- If a player
      draws a tile which can be immediately played, then it must be played.\n\n-
      If a player can not play a tile and can not draw from the boneyard either,
      then he/she simply passes the turn to the next player.\n\n- In All Fives only
      the first double is a spinner which means tiles can be placed on all four edges
      of the spinner double.\n\n- The round ends if a player has played all of his/her
      tiles. Round can also end if the game is blocked which means no tile can be
      played by any of the players.\n\n- After a player plays a tile, all open ends
      of the layout are totalled and if the total is a multiple of five (5, 10 etc.),
      then the player immediately scores that total.\n\n- Doubles at the end of an
      arm in the layout are worth its pips count. Once two tiles are placed on a
      double, its pip count is not considered in the total (even when the spinner
      has one or two edges available to place tiles).\n\n- The open ends of the arms
      on all four edges of the spinner double are counted in the score.\n\n- The
      first player playing all his/her tiles wins the round. If the game is blocked
      then the player with the lowest pips count in his/her hand wins.\n\n- After
      the round ends, pips on all remaining tiles of all players are counted and
      rounded up or down to the nearest multiple of five and then divided by five
      which is scored by the winner of that round.\n\n- The game is played for multiple
      rounds and the player with the highest score above 100 wins.\n\n- Though players
      score during the game play, the game can only end after the end of a round.
      It is possible that the first player to reach the score of 100 is not the winner
      if the round is won by another player who after adding the bonus points has
      the highest overall score."
  - id: concentrate
    spriteImg: {fileID: 21300000, guid: adfaa6081f909224f8b4911ff7a93da4, type: 3}
    linkVideos: []
    descriptionText: A memory-focused mode where players flip hidden tiles to find
      pairs that total twelve. Success rewards extra turns, challenging players to
      recall positions, chain accurate matches, and clear the board with the fewest
      attempts.
    rulesText: '- Uses a 28-tile double-six set or a 56 tile one.


      - This
      game is basically a memory test. The aim is to turn over all tiles in pairs
      so that the total pips sum 12.


      - If the turned over tiles do not add
      up to 12, then they are turned back down. Player can select two tiles again
      to repeat the process.


      - Tiles are removed in pairs and only if its
      total equals 12 pips.


      - Players who successfully remove a pair, take
      an extra turn and repeat this process, this happens again and again until the
      player fails to remove a pair which pips equal to 12.


      - Try to turn
      over all tiles in the least number of attempts.'
